  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1020 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nTuesday through Thursday...\n\nA deck of lower clouds crept into eastern Nebraska this evening, as \ntemperatures dipped into the 40s. Overnight, lows will only bottom \nout in the lower 40s and upper 30s, with cloud cover acting as a \nblanket, preventing significant radiational cooling. \n\nSoutherly winds will increase early Tuesday, as the pressure \ngradient tightens on thmorninge western end of the upper ridge.\nWinds up to 15 to 25 mph will be possible, as gusts reach \nspeeds of 30-40 mph. While we may briefly approach Wind Advisory\ncriteria in far northeast Nebraska Tuesday, the current \nforecast falls just shy. Sunnier skies and better mixing could \nbe the key to stronger winds on Wednesday, inching us a little \nbit closer to meeting Advisory thresholds. However, some \nuncertainty still remains. \n\nThe strong southwesterly flow will give temperatures a boost through \nmid week. Highs Tuesday could be tempered by some lingering cloud \ncover, but should still reach the mid to upper 50s. Wednesday most \nlocations should reach the low to mid 60s as cloud cover clears.  \n\nA surface low will push into the back side of the ridge and into the \nDakotas Wednesday, dragging a cold front across the forecast area \nWednesday evening. Model solutions continue to trend drier with the \nfrontal passage, with PoPs remaining below 10% for the forecast \narea. Southerly return flow behind the low to our north will help \nkeep temperatures from dropping much on Thursday. Expect highs to \nremain in the mid 50s to low 60s. \n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nAfter several dry days this week, our attention turns to precip \nchances Friday and Saturday as another trough and surface low push \nthrough the region. The surface low is expected to track from \nnortheast CO Friday morning, northeast through western NE during the \nday, and into the Dakotas by Saturday morning. With this farther \nnorth trend, expect precipitation to remain in the form of rain \nacross eastern Nebraska and southwest Iowa. Showers will develop \nover northeast Nebraska Friday evening, as warm air and moisture \nadvection ramp up (20-40% chance). Higher chances will arrive Friday \nnight into Saturday as the surface low drags a cold front through \nthe forecast area (40-60% chance). Overall the highest rain chances \nwill remain in northeast NE and west-central IA. While some timing \ndifferences remain, expect rain chances to diminish through the day \nSaturday, clearing the majority of the forecast area by the early \nevening. \n\nTemperatures will fall behind the cold front Saturday, bringing us a \ntouch cooler than normal for this time of year. Expect highs in the \nmid 60s on Friday falling into the upper 30s and low 40s by the end \nof the weekend.",
